\section*{Appendix A: Cryptographic Verification Framework}

\subsection*{A.1 Post-Quantum State Commitments}
All telemetry frames generated by the autonomous intelligence kernel are secured using
post-quantum ML-KEM commitments. Each simulation state $S_t$ is bound to a cryptographic
commitment $C_t$ such that:
\begin{equation}
C_t = \text{Commit}_{\text{ML-KEM}}(S_t, r_t),
\end{equation}
where $r_t$ is a randomly sampled blinding vector. This ensures tamper-evident state
transitions across decentralized execution grids.

\subsection*{A.2 Zero-Knowledge Verification}
State transitions are validated using zk-SNARK proofs. For each computational step
$S_t \rightarrow S_{t+1}$, a succinct proof $\pi_t$ is generated:
\begin{equation}
\pi_t = \text{SNARK.Prove}(S_t, S_{t+1}, \mathcal{R}),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}$ encodes the physical constraints of the simulation domain. Verifiers
can confirm correctness without accessing raw simulation data.

\subsection*{A.3 Homomorphic Encryption Layer (CKKS)}
All tensor operations are executed on encrypted data using the CKKS homomorphic scheme.
Given encrypted tensors $\text{Enc}(x)$ and $\text{Enc}(y)$, the execution kernel performs:
\begin{equation}
\text{Enc}(x + y), \quad \text{Enc}(x \cdot y),
\end{equation}
without decrypting intermediate states. This guarantees full data sovereignty during
multi-physics computation.

\subsection*{A.4 Ledger Integrity and State History}
A cryptographic ledger records all simulation states, commitments, and proofs:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = \{ (S_t, C_t, \pi_t) \mid t = 0, 1, \dots, T \}.
\end{equation}
The ledger ensures reproducibility and provides a verifiable audit trail for all
multi-messenger telemetry ingestion and autonomous correction cycles.

\subsection*{A.5 Security Guarantees}
The combined ML-KEM, zk-SNARK, and CKKS layers provide:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Forward-secure state commitments
    \item Zero-knowledge correctness verification
    \item Fully homomorphic encrypted computation
    \item Immutable execution history
\end{itemize}
These guarantees enable decentralized scientific computation without compromising
data integrity or sovereignty.